# converted from 02-the-bargain.md
@title The Bargain
@bg depths
@mood dread

narrate He waited four days.
narrate He would want that on the record, later, when there was nobody left to record anything. He did not go north on the first night, or the second, or the third. He tried everything a healer knows first. He tried the bark of the grey willow and the ash of the river reed and the paste they made in the drowned villages that nobody talked about. He tried a poultice that burned his own hands to make.
narrate He tried, for four days, to not need the hole in the field.
narrate On the fourth night Ilsa stopped recognising him for eleven minutes.
narrate She came back. She came back and she was frightened and she did not know why, and she held his hand and asked him whether the fever was worse, and he said no, and she believed him, and he went outside and stood in the garden and looked north and this time he did not go back inside.
narrate It was eleven miles.
narrate He knew the way. Every child in the Mire knew the way; you learned it the way you learned which mushrooms would kill you, by being told once, seriously, by an adult who was not joking.
narrate He walked it in three hours.
narrate The hole was six feet across and the grass went right up to the edge.
narrate That was the thing that had frightened him as a boy and that frightened him still: the tidiness of it. No scorch, no rot, no ring of dead ground. Just a hole in a field with grass around it, the way a well is a hole in a garden, the way a thing can be ordinary and be the end of you.
narrate He stood at the edge for a long time.
narrate And then he said, out loud, to the hole in the field:
corin neutral My daughter is dying.
narrate Nothing answered. He had not expected anything to answer. He had expected — and he had been right — that the asking was the thing, and that the answer would arrive inside him the way a decision does, and that he would know it had been accepted the way you know a door has closed behind you without turning round.
bryn neutral I want three days,
narrate He would spend twelve years trying to explain why he said that. He never managed it, and in the end he stopped trying, because every explanation was a lie of one kind or another and he had made himself a promise about lies.
narrate Here is what was true:
narrate He knew. Standing there, in the dark, with the grass moving, he knew exactly what he could have asked for. He had known it since the second mile of the walk back from the gate. He knew that the thing under the field was old and indifferent and literal, and that it did not measure requests by how much they cost the asker — it measured them by how much they were. He knew that if he said end the Grey Fever, everywhere, forever, for as long as there are people to catch it, it would be done. He knew it the way he knew the Fever's grey at the fingertips. He had no doubt at all.
narrate And he stood there, and he thought about forty thousand people in the Mire, and eleven thousand dead in the east, and every child who would ever wake up grey.
narrate And he said:
corin neutral I want three days.
narrate He would think that sentence every day for twelve years. He would think it in the morning and he would think it at night and he would think it standing in the ruins of villages he had burned.
narrate He would never once manage to make it sound like something a good man would think.
narrate The price arrived the way he had been told it would: not at a door, but everywhere at once.
narrate He felt it in his fingers first — a cold, and then a quietness, and then a sense of something being tidied, the way you tidy a room before guests. And he stood in the dark and felt his own mind being made neat, and he could not stop it, and he did not try.
narrate He would not know for a week what it had taken.
narrate That was the design, he understood later. You did not get to choose. You did not get to say take the bad ones. You paid in the currency you had, and the currency was everything you had ever gone back to, and the things you went back to most were the good ones.
narrate He lost four years.
narrate Not the Fever, not the funeral, not the fire. He lost the winter when she was four and made him a crown out of reeds. He lost the morning she learned to swim and the afternoon she fell out of the apple tree and the evening she asked him, very seriously, whether he thought the moon was lonely.
narrate He did not know he had lost them. That was the mercy, and it was the cruellest thing about it: you did not feel the holes. You simply found, months later, that someone could mention a thing that had happened to you and you had nothing on the other side of it. A story about your own life, second-hand.
narrate He walked back.
narrate The cold had settled into his right hand, and when he looked at his palm, there it was — a circle, and inside it a hand.
narrate He looked at it for a moment. Then he closed his fist and walked the eleven miles home, and got there at first light, and went inside.
narrate Ilsa was awake.
narrate She was sitting up.
narrate She looked at him, and her eyes were her eyes — the ones she'd had at four, and at six, and yesterday — and she said:
bryn neutral Da.
narrate And Cael — who had been preparing himself, for four days, to be strong in this moment, to be a physician, to be a man who could do the arithmetic — sat down on the floor beside her bed and put his head in his hands and wept like a child.
narrate She patted his hair.
corin neutral Don't be sad,
corin neutral You're always sad. It's your face.
